1
00:00:04,004 --> 00:00:04,804
‎TOKYO-HAKONE
‎ROUND-TRIP COLLEGE EKIDEN RACE

2
00:00:04,871 --> 00:00:07,807
The Hakone Ekiden was founded in 1920.

3
00:00:07,874 --> 00:00:09,676
Nice shot on Camera One!

4
00:00:09,743 --> 00:00:12,178
-At Dainichi TV, the crew…
-Show lineup. Graphics.

5
00:00:12,679 --> 00:00:16,416
-…led by chief producer Ryo Tokushige
-Ten seconds to air!

6
00:00:16,483 --> 00:00:18,952
was pressured by
ratings-focused executives

7
00:00:19,019 --> 00:00:21,521
to make the broadcast more entertaining.

8
00:00:21,588 --> 00:00:22,455
Idiot!

9
00:00:22,522 --> 00:00:25,625
The Hakone Ekiden doesn't belong to you!

10
00:00:25,692 --> 00:00:28,728
It's Dainichi TV's precious asset!

11
00:00:28,795 --> 00:00:30,096
It belongs to

12
00:00:30,163 --> 00:00:31,631
the runners.

13
00:00:31,698 --> 00:00:33,233
Tokushige's decision

14
00:00:33,299 --> 00:00:36,536
shook the entire network.

15
00:00:36,603 --> 00:00:39,539
At the sponsor's request
since the first broadcast,

16
00:00:39,606 --> 00:00:42,509
they were promised that the broadcast

17
00:00:42,575 --> 00:00:43,510
could continue as usual.

18
00:00:43,576 --> 00:00:44,677
Yeah!

19
00:00:44,744 --> 00:00:47,313
Meanwhile, Meisei Gakuin failed to qualify

20
00:00:47,380 --> 00:00:50,917
after captain Hayato Aoba faded.

21
00:00:50,984 --> 00:00:52,619
Legendary coach Hisashige Moroya named

22
00:00:52,685 --> 00:00:54,988
inexperienced Masato Kai

23
00:00:55,055 --> 00:00:56,923
as his successor.

24
00:00:56,990 --> 00:00:58,358
We're going back to Hakone.

25
00:00:58,425 --> 00:01:00,627
That's my only goal.

26
00:01:00,693 --> 00:01:03,129
In an unusual move,
Kai was also put in charge of

27
00:01:03,196 --> 00:01:05,865
the Kanto Student United Team

28
00:01:05,932 --> 00:01:06,800
Can you do it?

29
00:01:06,866 --> 00:01:08,034
I can.

30
00:01:08,601 --> 00:01:11,171
The battle of runners
who had already tasted defeat

31
00:01:11,237 --> 00:01:13,440
was about to begin.

32
00:01:21,147 --> 00:01:24,417
STUDENT UNITED TEAM MEETING DAY

33
00:01:24,484 --> 00:01:25,418
THE STUDENT UNITED TEAM

34
00:01:25,485 --> 00:01:27,253
THE 21ST TEAM OF RUNNERS
WHO FAILED TO QUALIFY

35
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:28,488
CAMP AT TOHO ECONOMICS

36
00:01:28,555 --> 00:01:29,389
It's finally time.

37
00:01:29,956 --> 00:01:31,391
MEISEI GAKUIN CAPTAIN
HAYATO AOBA

38
00:01:31,458 --> 00:01:32,992
The 10,000-kilometer…

39
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:35,161
You mean 10,000 meters.

40
00:01:35,228 --> 00:01:37,831
That's Keisuke Nogi,
a first-year at Keisei University…

41
00:01:37,897 --> 00:01:39,165
-You said 10,000 kilometers.
-No.

42
00:01:39,232 --> 00:01:40,600
…and Daichi Murai, a third-year

43
00:01:40,667 --> 00:01:42,368
at Toho University of Economics.

44
00:01:42,936 --> 00:01:45,638
They're the top runners
with the two best times.

45
00:01:48,842 --> 00:01:50,310
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL
KOTA MATSUKI

46
00:01:50,376 --> 00:01:51,911
KANTO UNIVERSITY OF CULTURE
SEIYA NAITO/HARUKA MOMOYAMA/SHUTO TOMIOKA

47
00:01:51,978 --> 00:01:53,346
I hear some runners

48
00:01:53,913 --> 00:01:56,749
were reluctant
to join the Student United Team.

49
00:01:57,550 --> 00:01:58,585
-Really?
-Yes.

50
00:01:58,651 --> 00:01:59,486
Morning.

51
00:01:59,552 --> 00:02:01,821
-Good morning!
-Good morning!

52
00:02:04,791 --> 00:02:08,194
I'm Kai, head coach
of the Student United Team.

53
00:02:09,929 --> 00:02:11,064
Nice to meet you.

54
00:02:11,131 --> 00:02:14,534
I'm Onuma, one of your coaches,
from Toho University of Economics.

55
00:02:14,601 --> 00:02:16,603
Let's run an exciting race together.

56
00:02:16,669 --> 00:02:19,372
Feel free to make full use
of our facilities.

57
00:02:19,939 --> 00:02:23,176
I'm Kitano, also a coach,
from Seiwa International University.

58
00:02:23,243 --> 00:02:24,110
KOICHI KITANO

59
00:02:24,177 --> 00:02:26,946
Let's make this a good experience
that helps each of your teams.

60
00:02:27,013 --> 00:02:28,748
-Nice to meet you.
-Nice to meet you.

61
00:02:32,585 --> 00:02:34,988
Tenma Isayama, fourth-year,
Shinagawa Institute of Technology.

62
00:02:35,054 --> 00:02:35,889
TENMA ISAYAMA

63
00:02:35,955 --> 00:02:38,658
If I'm running Hakone,
I want to be on TV and stand out!

64
00:02:38,725 --> 00:02:40,126
That's all I'm here for!

65
00:02:42,328 --> 00:02:43,163
Nice to meet you.

66
00:02:43,229 --> 00:02:46,299
I'm Jo Inomata, a second-year
at Musashino Agricultural University.

67
00:02:46,366 --> 00:02:49,235
I'm 190 cm tall,
and I'm confident in my leg strength.

68
00:02:49,936 --> 00:02:50,904
Nice to meet you.

69
00:02:50,970 --> 00:02:52,305
Nice to meet you.

70
00:02:52,805 --> 00:02:55,441
I'm Kota Matsuki, a fourth-year
at Seiwa International University.

71
00:02:59,913 --> 00:03:00,747
Well…

72
00:03:02,715 --> 00:03:05,485
I hope to gain experience
I can take back to my own team.

73
00:03:05,552 --> 00:03:06,386
Nice to meet you.

74
00:03:09,055 --> 00:03:10,323
Nice to meet you.

75
00:03:11,424 --> 00:03:12,659
Next, Aoba.

76
00:03:13,660 --> 00:03:14,494
Yes.

77
00:03:18,064 --> 00:03:18,898
I don't want

78
00:03:20,567 --> 00:03:22,202
the Student United Team

79
00:03:23,503 --> 00:03:26,005
to be just about gaining experience.

80
00:03:27,840 --> 00:03:29,943
Every other team

81
00:03:30,009 --> 00:03:31,911
will be going all out to win.

82
00:03:34,247 --> 00:03:35,548
I want us to be one team

83
00:03:36,749 --> 00:03:38,718
and truly compete against our rivals.

84
00:03:39,686 --> 00:03:40,687
That's what I believe.

85
00:03:52,999 --> 00:03:53,866
Oh, sorry.

86
00:03:56,569 --> 00:03:57,637
I'm Dan Kurashina,

87
00:03:57,704 --> 00:03:58,538
‎SECOND-YEAR AT SANNO UNIVERSITY
‎DAN KURASHINA

88
00:03:58,605 --> 00:04:00,006
a second-year at Sanno University.

89
00:04:00,840 --> 00:04:02,709
I wanted to run Hakone no matter what,

90
00:04:03,509 --> 00:04:06,446
so I'm real happy I was selected!

91
00:04:07,380 --> 00:04:08,281
Let's make these

92
00:04:08,348 --> 00:04:09,983
two months count!

93
00:04:11,551 --> 00:04:12,485
Nice to meet you.

94
00:04:15,455 --> 00:04:16,856
That's all 16 runners.

95
00:04:17,657 --> 00:04:20,460
Ten of you will run Hakone.

96
00:04:26,332 --> 00:04:28,735
Yano, anything to say?

97
00:04:29,736 --> 00:04:30,603
Yes.

98
00:04:31,671 --> 00:04:33,306
Keito Yano from Meisei Gakuin University.

99
00:04:33,373 --> 00:04:35,275
I'll be the team manager.

100
00:04:37,777 --> 00:04:39,178
I have one suggestion.

101
00:04:39,746 --> 00:04:41,648
Why don't we call each other
by our first names?

102
00:04:42,382 --> 00:04:44,350
I think it would help us feel closer.

103
00:04:45,118 --> 00:04:47,720
It might be a little awkward at first,
but…

104
00:04:48,921 --> 00:04:49,956
what does everyone think?

105
00:04:50,023 --> 00:04:51,858
-I'm in!
-Then

106
00:04:52,458 --> 00:04:55,261
call me Makko-chan!

107
00:04:55,862 --> 00:04:57,063
Please call me

108
00:04:57,630 --> 00:04:59,198
Takkun!

109
00:05:00,199 --> 00:05:01,200
Thank you.

110
00:05:01,901 --> 00:05:04,704
Next, I'd like to choose a captain.

111
00:05:05,371 --> 00:05:06,873
Any volunteers?

112
00:05:31,030 --> 00:05:31,998
I'll volunteer.

113
00:05:36,803 --> 00:05:37,937
Thank you.

114
00:05:41,641 --> 00:05:42,842
Lastly,

115
00:05:43,543 --> 00:05:45,978
what is this Student United Team
running for?

116
00:05:47,380 --> 00:05:50,516
To make that clear, I want to set a goal.

117
00:05:52,685 --> 00:05:53,786
My goal is

118
00:05:54,987 --> 00:05:56,789
a top-three finish at the Hakone Ekiden.

119
00:06:01,327 --> 00:06:02,462
Top three?

120
00:06:03,029 --> 00:06:04,063
Seriously?

121
00:06:05,031 --> 00:06:06,165
As you know,

122
00:06:06,232 --> 00:06:09,836
the Student United Team is a non-scoring
entry. Our times aren't official.

123
00:06:11,771 --> 00:06:14,907
Even if we won,
it would be a phantom victory.

124
00:06:16,175 --> 00:06:19,846
Still, the reason I've set this goal

125
00:06:20,646 --> 00:06:23,049
is that you gain nothing
from a race you don't run for real.

126
00:06:25,318 --> 00:06:27,387
If you just want a taste of Hakone,

127
00:06:27,887 --> 00:06:29,756
just wave a flag by the side of the road.

128
00:06:32,125 --> 00:06:34,560
See you at the track in 30 minutes.

129
00:06:36,329 --> 00:06:37,397
-Yes.
-Yes.

130
00:06:44,070 --> 00:06:47,206
56 DAYS UNTIL THE HAKONE EKIDEN

131
00:06:47,273 --> 00:06:49,809
There are too many captions
for Keya's part. Watch that.

132
00:06:50,309 --> 00:06:53,279
Also, we interviewed Murakami,
who couldn't run because of injury.

133
00:06:53,346 --> 00:06:54,213
We need to use that.

134
00:06:54,280 --> 00:06:55,248
Understood.

135
00:06:55,314 --> 00:06:56,849
I'll give you notes on the rest.

136
00:06:57,417 --> 00:06:58,718
I'm heading to the CG meeting.

137
00:07:00,386 --> 00:07:03,322
ASSISTANT DIRECTOR
CHIKA TOYAMA

138
00:07:03,389 --> 00:07:04,991
Mr. Nomura,

139
00:07:05,625 --> 00:07:09,762
this is my fourth round of fixes
on the website video.

140
00:07:11,397 --> 00:07:13,099
Is it really that bad?

141
00:07:13,599 --> 00:07:16,969
"Hakone Highlights to Study Up On."

142
00:07:17,036 --> 00:07:18,671
-So this is what you're doing?
-Yes.

143
00:07:18,738 --> 00:07:19,839
Yes.

144
00:07:19,906 --> 00:07:21,774
-Everyone's shaping up.
-Yeah.

145
00:07:21,841 --> 00:07:24,343
Maybe add captions
to make it easier to follow?

146
00:07:24,410 --> 00:07:27,447
No, Natsuki doesn't seem
to like captions much.

147
00:07:30,783 --> 00:07:32,985
Then hype it up with narration?

148
00:07:33,052 --> 00:07:36,556
She told me not to use
too much narration either.

149
00:07:39,892 --> 00:07:41,928
CHIEF PRODUCER
RYO TOKUSHIGE

150
00:07:41,994 --> 00:07:43,830
…15, 16,

151
00:07:44,430 --> 00:07:45,798
17, 18,

152
00:07:46,299 --> 00:07:49,168
19, 20, 21.

153
00:07:49,735 --> 00:07:51,170
It's a good pace!

154
00:07:51,804 --> 00:07:53,139
Stay relaxed!

155
00:07:58,010 --> 00:08:00,813
Those two really are fast…

156
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:02,381
KEISUKE NOGI / DAICHI MURAI

157
00:08:02,448 --> 00:08:03,549
Lap 25,

158
00:08:03,616 --> 00:08:05,284
26, 27.

159
00:08:05,952 --> 00:08:06,786
Kota, pick it up!

160
00:08:07,353 --> 00:08:09,222
SEIWA INTERNATIONAL UNIVERSITY
KOTA MATSUKI

161
00:08:12,725 --> 00:08:14,527
Just keep that pace.

162
00:08:19,298 --> 00:08:20,900
Haruka! Stay! Don't speed up now!

163
00:08:21,467 --> 00:08:24,971
THIRD-YEAR AT TOYO UNIVERSITY OF COMMERCE
HARUKA MOMOYAMA

164
00:08:25,037 --> 00:08:27,707
…35, 36,

165
00:08:27,773 --> 00:08:30,376
37, 38, 39,

166
00:08:31,010 --> 00:08:33,513
40, 41, 42,

167
00:08:34,313 --> 00:08:36,349
43, 44…

168
00:08:36,415 --> 00:08:37,683
Nice.

169
00:08:48,261 --> 00:08:52,632
…46, 47, 48…

170
00:09:00,373 --> 00:09:02,775
You only get one shot.

171
00:09:07,380 --> 00:09:10,116
Also, send me your draft questions
for the runner interviews.

172
00:09:10,182 --> 00:09:12,385
I want to check them beforehand.

173
00:09:13,419 --> 00:09:14,587
She sure doesn't trust us.

174
00:09:14,654 --> 00:09:15,555
‎ASSISTANT DIRECTOR
‎AYUMU HASHIGUCHI

175
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:16,889
-Mr. Kuwana.
-Yes.

176
00:09:16,956 --> 00:09:20,393
I need that camera operator list
for the broadcast points soon.

177
00:09:20,459 --> 00:09:21,661
I know!

178
00:09:22,228 --> 00:09:24,564
-We've got a lot going on too.
-Right?

179
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:26,132
That's it for today's meeting.

180
00:09:26,198 --> 00:09:28,467
-Okay.
-Okay.

181
00:09:30,369 --> 00:09:31,904
Oh, Toyama. Here.

182
00:09:32,538 --> 00:09:34,473
-What's this?
-Fixes for that video segment.

183
00:09:35,508 --> 00:09:36,976
What, all of this?

184
00:09:37,543 --> 00:09:39,946
"Cut two. Too close on the runner's face."
"Cut three,

185
00:09:40,012 --> 00:09:42,615
trim one second off the top.
Cut four. Use a wider shot."

186
00:09:44,717 --> 00:09:46,953
I'm basically redoing the whole thing!

187
00:09:47,019 --> 00:09:47,887
Hey, let me see that.

188
00:09:50,957 --> 00:09:54,260
If this were for broadcast, sure.
But it's just a video for the website.

189
00:09:54,994 --> 00:09:56,562
Why not let her do it her way?

190
00:09:57,263 --> 00:09:59,599
If you shoot down everything,
she'll lose her motivation.

191
00:09:59,665 --> 00:10:02,668
I'm just pointing out what isn't working.

192
00:10:02,735 --> 00:10:03,569
Hey.

193
00:10:04,337 --> 00:10:07,640
I'm saying don't do everything your way.

194
00:10:07,707 --> 00:10:09,575
Try trusting other people for once.

195
00:10:10,710 --> 00:10:12,078
There's no right answer here.

196
00:10:14,780 --> 00:10:18,451
If everyone does whatever they want,
the team won't come together.

197
00:10:18,951 --> 00:10:21,587
That's what the broadcast director
is there for, I think.

198
00:10:21,654 --> 00:10:22,722
So what,

199
00:10:23,255 --> 00:10:25,658
we have to follow whatever you say?

200
00:10:26,459 --> 00:10:28,127
Is the broadcast director
some kind of god?

201
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:35,701
RAMEN

202
00:10:35,768 --> 00:10:37,103
I see…

203
00:10:37,770 --> 00:10:40,806
Hakone's less than two months away.
This is really bad.

204
00:10:41,374 --> 00:10:43,643
So, what do you think?

205
00:10:43,709 --> 00:10:44,744
DIRECTOR
TOUGO MATSUZAWA

206
00:10:44,810 --> 00:10:46,278
-Me?
-Yeah.

207
00:10:48,014 --> 00:10:49,215
Well,

208
00:10:49,849 --> 00:10:52,718
I've known Ms. Miyamoto a long time,

209
00:10:52,785 --> 00:10:54,920
so I'm kind of used to it…

210
00:10:54,987 --> 00:10:58,257
And she has her good points…

211
00:10:58,324 --> 00:11:02,728
But honestly,
I get where Mr. Nomura's coming from.

212
00:11:02,795 --> 00:11:05,131
Ms. Miyamoto's super-talented,

213
00:11:05,197 --> 00:11:07,166
but that makes her hard on people.

214
00:11:07,733 --> 00:11:10,803
If she praised us a little more,
we'd be more motivated.

215
00:11:10,870 --> 00:11:11,971
That's what I think.

216
00:11:12,038 --> 00:11:14,440
Would it kill her

217
00:11:14,507 --> 00:11:15,908
to be a bit nicer

218
00:11:15,975 --> 00:11:19,278
with greetings and email replies?

219
00:11:19,345 --> 00:11:20,179
And another thing…

220
00:11:20,813 --> 00:11:22,348
I can't believe we have camp

221
00:11:22,415 --> 00:11:23,916
every single weekend!

222
00:11:24,650 --> 00:11:28,187
Usually the Student United Team meets
only two or three times before the race.

223
00:11:28,254 --> 00:11:29,555
So this is pretty rare.

224
00:11:30,756 --> 00:11:32,858
But I'm glad we get to train a lot.

225
00:11:33,359 --> 00:11:34,360
Good work.

226
00:11:34,927 --> 00:11:36,028
Are you a manga character?

227
00:11:36,095 --> 00:11:38,097
Running makes me hungry.

228
00:11:38,764 --> 00:11:40,199
Let's eat.

229
00:11:40,266 --> 00:11:41,333
LEG PREFERENCE SURVEY

230
00:11:41,400 --> 00:11:43,502
Oh, the leg preference survey!

231
00:11:43,569 --> 00:11:45,337
Which leg does everyone want?

232
00:11:47,573 --> 00:11:48,407
For me,

233
00:11:48,474 --> 00:11:51,377
maybe the fifth or sixth leg.
I'm good on hills.

234
00:11:51,444 --> 00:11:52,244
‎FIFTH LEG-ODAWARA TO LAKE ASHI
‎SIXTH LEG-LAKE ASHI TO ODAWARA

235
00:11:52,311 --> 00:11:55,281
Keisuke and Daichi are the favorites
for the prestigious second leg.

236
00:11:55,347 --> 00:11:57,183
Look at you, top runners!

237
00:11:57,249 --> 00:11:58,551
Well, I don't know.

238
00:11:58,617 --> 00:12:00,820
I'll do my best. It's my dream.

239
00:12:01,454 --> 00:12:04,156
-Tenma, which leg do you want?
-The first leg and the third leg.

240
00:12:04,657 --> 00:12:07,093
The start of the first leg
is always on TV.

241
00:12:07,660 --> 00:12:10,396
If I'm on TV longer,
my parents will be happy.

242
00:12:11,063 --> 00:12:12,431
And I can brag to my friends.

243
00:12:12,498 --> 00:12:14,133
Talk about shallow motives.

244
00:12:14,700 --> 00:12:17,002
Then the tenth leg at the end
is always on TV too.

245
00:12:17,069 --> 00:12:18,070
Roadside crowds are huge.

246
00:12:18,137 --> 00:12:22,007
In the return race,
they only film you if you're up front.

247
00:12:22,074 --> 00:12:23,676
Plus, we're the Student United Team.

248
00:12:23,743 --> 00:12:25,144
So why the third leg?

249
00:12:25,711 --> 00:12:28,414
The view, of course!

250
00:12:29,115 --> 00:12:31,117
The Shonan Coast. Mount Fuji.

251
00:12:31,183 --> 00:12:33,385
-It's the best!
-That's why?

252
00:12:33,452 --> 00:12:34,987
Of course!

253
00:12:36,288 --> 00:12:37,523
Hey! What are you doing?

254
00:12:38,090 --> 00:12:38,958
What about you guys?

255
00:12:39,525 --> 00:12:40,626
Any legs you want?

256
00:12:42,128 --> 00:12:45,798
Whatever.
We just run the leg we're assigned.

257
00:12:46,832 --> 00:12:48,334
I agree.

258
00:12:48,400 --> 00:12:51,170
Yeah, same for me, pretty much.

259
00:12:52,371 --> 00:12:53,439
Hey.

260
00:12:53,506 --> 00:12:55,407
You said the third leg has a nice view,

261
00:12:55,975 --> 00:12:57,810
but it's a pretty tough leg.

262
00:12:58,577 --> 00:13:02,314
The first half is a gentle downhill,
but along the coast, the wind hits you.

263
00:13:02,381 --> 00:13:05,851
Temperatures swing a lot. Your legs
could give out if you're careless.

264
00:13:07,586 --> 00:13:10,022
Hope you have time to look at Mount Fuji.

265
00:13:10,089 --> 00:13:12,992
It'd be the worst if you got dizzy

266
00:13:13,926 --> 00:13:15,194
and couldn't pass the sash.

267
00:13:15,928 --> 00:13:16,896
Well, see you.

268
00:13:19,865 --> 00:13:21,167
What was that?

269
00:13:24,336 --> 00:13:25,304
TOHO ECONOMICS

270
00:13:25,371 --> 00:13:26,338
Still,

271
00:13:26,405 --> 00:13:29,508
I was surprised by the top-three goal.

272
00:13:30,342 --> 00:13:34,013
Come to think of it,
Coach Kai saw right through me.

273
00:13:34,914 --> 00:13:37,249
Today I hesitated over
when to make my move.

274
00:13:39,819 --> 00:13:41,854
You only get one shot.

275
00:13:43,122 --> 00:13:44,924
It was the same when
we picked the captain.

276
00:13:44,990 --> 00:13:46,625
Any volunteers?

277
00:13:49,361 --> 00:13:50,896
Coach and I made eye contact.

278
00:13:51,697 --> 00:13:54,333
And before I knew it, I'd raised my hand.

279
00:13:57,336 --> 00:13:58,437
I didn't plan to volunteer.

280
00:14:01,674 --> 00:14:04,410
But I was really happy

281
00:14:04,944 --> 00:14:07,012
that you volunteered to be captain.

282
00:14:08,013 --> 00:14:08,981
Thanks.

283
00:14:12,051 --> 00:14:16,622
But everyone's kind of all over the place.

284
00:14:19,825 --> 00:14:21,527
Can I really do this?

285
00:14:26,966 --> 00:14:29,368
I don't think it'll be easy.

286
00:14:30,135 --> 00:14:34,573
But I think you're the only one
who can do it.

287
00:14:35,140 --> 00:14:37,676
I'll support the team
with everything I have.

288
00:14:38,744 --> 00:14:39,578
Okay.

289
00:14:40,279 --> 00:14:42,781
What the heck?

290
00:14:42,848 --> 00:14:46,151
No one's filling in their work schedules.
Seriously…

291
00:14:46,719 --> 00:14:48,654
Well,

292
00:14:48,721 --> 00:14:50,890
I guess this is part of

293
00:14:50,956 --> 00:14:52,758
a chief producer's job too…

294
00:14:53,993 --> 00:14:56,328
Mr. Tokushige, you always used to say

295
00:14:56,395 --> 00:14:57,930
the field was where you belonged.

296
00:14:59,131 --> 00:15:01,901
Running around in the field was fun.

297
00:15:02,501 --> 00:15:06,272
But once you're in management,
there's a lot to deal with.

298
00:15:06,839 --> 00:15:08,774
Even when I get home exhausted,

299
00:15:09,275 --> 00:15:11,143
all a divorced guy like me has waiting

300
00:15:11,210 --> 00:15:14,580
is a lonely can of beer.

301
00:15:20,219 --> 00:15:21,086
Mr. Tokushige.

302
00:15:24,056 --> 00:15:26,992
Have you ever wanted to quit your job?

303
00:15:27,893 --> 00:15:29,194
Of course.

304
00:15:30,195 --> 00:15:32,097
I think about it every day.

305
00:15:35,067 --> 00:15:36,068
But

306
00:15:37,169 --> 00:15:40,873
in the end, I love this job.

307
00:15:43,108 --> 00:15:46,512
What about you? Ever wanted to quit?

308
00:15:49,248 --> 00:15:53,252
Maybe I'm not cut out for this job.

309
00:15:53,319 --> 00:15:54,520
What?

310
00:15:55,721 --> 00:15:57,556
Ever since I was a kid,

311
00:15:57,623 --> 00:16:00,626
I've been bad at working with others

312
00:16:01,160 --> 00:16:02,795
or bringing people together.

313
00:16:04,363 --> 00:16:06,765
I wish I could do it like you.

314
00:16:08,167 --> 00:16:09,068
I'm just good at

315
00:16:09,702 --> 00:16:11,570
finding people I trust

316
00:16:11,637 --> 00:16:12,871
and tossing things

317
00:16:13,639 --> 00:16:14,573
their way.

318
00:16:16,608 --> 00:16:17,810
"Toss-It-All Tokushige."

319
00:16:19,778 --> 00:16:21,246
That's what they used to call you.

320
00:16:21,880 --> 00:16:22,982
Did they?

321
00:16:23,682 --> 00:16:25,317
When you were broadcast director

322
00:16:25,384 --> 00:16:27,486
and I was the director in Car One.

323
00:16:30,155 --> 00:16:32,591
Miyamoto! The lead's changing!

324
00:16:32,658 --> 00:16:34,059
Stay on Car One for a while!

325
00:16:34,893 --> 00:16:37,029
It's all yours!

326
00:16:39,431 --> 00:16:40,265
Yes.

327
00:16:40,833 --> 00:16:42,434
Car One had three cameras,

328
00:16:43,135 --> 00:16:44,703
and I panicked over

329
00:16:45,304 --> 00:16:46,772
which one to take.

330
00:16:49,308 --> 00:16:50,275
But…

331
00:16:51,477 --> 00:16:52,444
I was

332
00:16:53,212 --> 00:16:54,813
really happy then.

333
00:16:57,182 --> 00:16:58,417
Still…

334
00:17:00,252 --> 00:17:03,856
trusting others takes courage.

335
00:17:06,191 --> 00:17:07,092
Does it?

336
00:17:08,394 --> 00:17:12,498
I tend to expect a lot from people.

337
00:17:13,499 --> 00:17:14,333
Because

338
00:17:14,867 --> 00:17:16,902
everyone has something I don't.

339
00:17:18,837 --> 00:17:19,805
Miyamoto,

340
00:17:20,572 --> 00:17:22,541
maybe don't toss it all,

341
00:17:24,109 --> 00:17:25,444
but try tossing a little?

342
00:17:25,511 --> 00:17:26,879
Toss a little?

343
00:17:31,150 --> 00:17:35,287
What? Matsuzawa hasn't taken
any of his comp days…

344
00:17:35,988 --> 00:17:36,822
‎HEAD COACH'S OFFICE

345
00:17:36,889 --> 00:17:37,790
What is it?

346
00:17:37,856 --> 00:17:40,025
There's something
I'd like to talk to you about.

347
00:17:47,900 --> 00:17:49,068
Some of the runners

348
00:17:49,701 --> 00:17:52,604
don't seem very into this camp.

349
00:17:53,372 --> 00:17:54,506
In today's practice,

350
00:17:54,573 --> 00:17:57,276
they didn't seem to be running for real.

351
00:17:59,645 --> 00:18:01,013
One of them has

352
00:18:01,080 --> 00:18:04,450
a 10,000-meter personal best
in the 28-minute range.

353
00:18:04,516 --> 00:18:07,686
They should've been competing
near the front today.

354
00:18:09,254 --> 00:18:10,823
Should I talk to them?

355
00:18:12,891 --> 00:18:14,326
Running is interesting.

356
00:18:16,195 --> 00:18:19,198
What's buried deep in your heart
shows itself.

357
00:18:21,200 --> 00:18:23,402
The team has only just started.

358
00:18:27,039 --> 00:18:28,273
There's still time.

359
00:18:37,249 --> 00:18:38,350
That's the length.

360
00:18:39,718 --> 00:18:40,919
Toyama.

361
00:18:41,820 --> 00:18:42,988
Oh, are we done?

362
00:18:43,055 --> 00:18:44,590
Then I'll go buy lunch!

363
00:18:44,656 --> 00:18:46,125
That's not it.

364
00:18:46,191 --> 00:18:48,093
-What do you think?
-Think?

365
00:18:49,027 --> 00:18:49,895
The video segment.

366
00:18:51,096 --> 00:18:51,930
What?

367
00:18:52,965 --> 00:18:53,999
No…

368
00:18:56,068 --> 00:18:56,935
It's perfect.

369
00:18:58,437 --> 00:18:59,805
You can say what you really think.

370
00:19:00,806 --> 00:19:01,673
What?

371
00:19:05,777 --> 00:19:06,812
Well…

372
00:19:08,947 --> 00:19:11,850
if I had to say,

373
00:19:12,751 --> 00:19:15,921
the lead-up to the qualifying race results

374
00:19:17,289 --> 00:19:20,159
feels a bit short.

375
00:19:20,225 --> 00:19:23,162
I kind of want to stay
on the edge of my seat longer.

376
00:19:24,863 --> 00:19:26,331
Give it a try.

377
00:19:27,232 --> 00:19:28,200
Give what a try?

378
00:19:28,267 --> 00:19:31,403
Cut it so it's more suspenseful.

379
00:19:31,470 --> 00:19:33,238
Me?

380
00:19:33,305 --> 00:19:36,341
The results will be announced shortly.

381
00:19:40,512 --> 00:19:41,346
Um…

382
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:44,683
-Can you hold this shot a little longer?
-Okay.

383
00:19:48,120 --> 00:19:50,389
I extended it by two seconds.

384
00:19:52,558 --> 00:19:55,260
Um, I think it's done.

385
00:19:56,795 --> 00:19:59,598
It's not suspenseful. It just drags.

386
00:20:00,732 --> 00:20:02,868
There's too much buildup
before the announcement,

387
00:20:02,935 --> 00:20:05,070
so the key part afterward falls flat.

388
00:20:05,137 --> 00:20:07,539
And then what we want viewers to see…

389
00:20:09,007 --> 00:20:10,375
Sorry…

390
00:20:14,780 --> 00:20:16,048
Let's have lunch.

391
00:20:19,184 --> 00:20:20,018
Sorry,

392
00:20:20,085 --> 00:20:21,954
the rice balls were sold out…

393
00:20:23,655 --> 00:20:26,992
NATTO ROLL

394
00:20:27,059 --> 00:20:28,660
Natto Roll.

395
00:20:29,962 --> 00:20:32,931
-Sorry?
-My nickname in elementary school.

396
00:20:34,299 --> 00:20:35,367
Natto Roll?

397
00:20:35,934 --> 00:20:36,802
Yeah.

398
00:20:38,437 --> 00:20:41,240
Um, why Natto Roll?

399
00:20:43,075 --> 00:20:45,477
-At first, it was Iron Mask.
-Iron Mask?

400
00:20:46,178 --> 00:20:47,412
Because I wasn't friendly.

401
00:20:49,014 --> 00:20:51,383
Somehow that became Tekka Roll,

402
00:20:52,284 --> 00:20:54,987
and then it evolved into Natto Roll.

403
00:20:55,053 --> 00:20:56,388
Evolved…

404
00:20:57,189 --> 00:20:58,557
You know how that goes, right?

405
00:20:58,624 --> 00:21:01,093
Do I…?

406
00:21:04,363 --> 00:21:05,297
So,

407
00:21:05,897 --> 00:21:08,533
you've been an Iron Mask
since you were a kid.

408
00:21:10,836 --> 00:21:12,004
Oh, sorry.

409
00:21:15,507 --> 00:21:18,210
Old habits die hard, I guess…

410
00:21:19,544 --> 00:21:21,747
Um, did something happen?

411
00:21:21,813 --> 00:21:23,749
You're acting weird today.

412
00:21:25,684 --> 00:21:27,552
-Let's eat.
-Okay.

413
00:21:33,859 --> 00:21:35,627
Eyes straight ahead!

414
00:21:35,694 --> 00:21:37,262
Don't let the pace drop!

415
00:21:37,329 --> 00:21:38,630
Um, that's…

416
00:21:38,697 --> 00:21:40,532
Yeah. Coach Nagura.

417
00:21:40,599 --> 00:21:45,103
He's in his 20th year as head coach
and led Kanto University to five titles.

418
00:21:45,170 --> 00:21:46,371
Mr. Maeda!

419
00:21:46,938 --> 00:21:48,340
Long time no see!

420
00:21:48,407 --> 00:21:50,409
Hey, it's been a while. How are you doing?

421
00:21:50,475 --> 00:21:52,210
-Not bad.
-Oh.

422
00:21:52,878 --> 00:21:54,780
More importantly,
did you get a little older?

423
00:21:54,846 --> 00:21:56,148
Hey,

424
00:21:56,214 --> 00:21:58,550
that's harsh right off the bat, Ono!

425
00:21:58,617 --> 00:22:00,052
Wow, they really love him.

426
00:22:00,819 --> 00:22:02,654
Mr. Maeda's research is on another level.

427
00:22:03,221 --> 00:22:05,691
From the finer points of the relay
to each runner's personality,

428
00:22:06,291 --> 00:22:08,260
no one matches him in volume or depth.

429
00:22:08,760 --> 00:22:11,630
That's why he's been
the broadcast announcer for six years.

430
00:22:11,697 --> 00:22:13,131
I see.

431
00:22:14,733 --> 00:22:15,667
Toyama.

432
00:22:15,734 --> 00:22:16,568
Yes?

433
00:22:16,635 --> 00:22:18,737
Do you know how many announcers
work on Hakone?

434
00:22:20,072 --> 00:22:21,173
Um…

435
00:22:23,075 --> 00:22:24,142
Twenty-four.

436
00:22:25,344 --> 00:22:28,146
And every one of them
trusts him completely.

437
00:22:28,213 --> 00:22:30,515
Right… Coach…

438
00:22:30,582 --> 00:22:32,384
With him as broadcast announcer,

439
00:22:33,051 --> 00:22:36,088
we can broadcast Hakone with confidence.

440
00:22:36,154 --> 00:22:37,589
Wow.

441
00:22:38,357 --> 00:22:41,760
Hakone's amazing.
Twenty-four announcers alone.

442
00:22:42,894 --> 00:22:44,496
How many staff are there in total?

443
00:22:44,563 --> 00:22:48,767
When I was about your age,
I was in charge of ordering staff lunches.

444
00:22:48,834 --> 00:22:51,470
Even back then it was 900 meals,
so there must be more people now.

445
00:22:51,536 --> 00:22:53,071
Nine hundred?

446
00:22:53,138 --> 00:22:55,474
Hakone is an insanely huge broadcast.

447
00:22:55,540 --> 00:22:56,641
But, yeah.

448
00:22:56,708 --> 00:22:59,444
It's reassuring to think
there are 900 pros working on it.

449
00:23:05,751 --> 00:23:06,752
Last 2,000!

450
00:23:07,319 --> 00:23:09,421
-Build it up!
-Focus!

451
00:23:09,488 --> 00:23:12,224
-Focus!
-One lap, 3:20.

452
00:23:18,530 --> 00:23:19,698
Let's pick it up from here!

453
00:23:20,265 --> 00:23:21,333
Almost there!

454
00:23:27,639 --> 00:23:30,108
-A press release?
-Yes.

455
00:23:30,175 --> 00:23:33,011
I want to tell the media
that the Student United Team

456
00:23:33,078 --> 00:23:34,212
has started training.

457
00:23:35,447 --> 00:23:37,816
Wouldn't it be better
not to do anything too flashy?

458
00:23:39,718 --> 00:23:41,887
I want to draw even a little attention

459
00:23:41,953 --> 00:23:43,455
and boost the runners' morale.

460
00:23:44,256 --> 00:23:47,125
I want to do everything I can for that.

461
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:50,562
If you really want to boost morale,

462
00:23:50,629 --> 00:23:54,299
wouldn't it be smarter
to set a more realistic goal?

463
00:23:56,301 --> 00:23:57,803
Top three.

464
00:23:58,737 --> 00:23:59,805
With a goal like that,

465
00:23:59,871 --> 00:24:03,141
it's no wonder
some runners feel they can't keep up.

466
00:24:05,377 --> 00:24:07,379
It's completely unrealistic.

467
00:24:07,879 --> 00:24:09,247
In the first place,

468
00:24:09,781 --> 00:24:12,384
winning isn't even the point
of the Student United Team.

469
00:24:13,985 --> 00:24:16,354
I can't support training that puts

470
00:24:16,421 --> 00:24:17,989
needless strain on the runners.

471
00:24:19,858 --> 00:24:22,093
Well, there are all sorts of opinions,

472
00:24:23,094 --> 00:24:25,230
but Kai is the head coach
of the Student United Team.

473
00:24:25,897 --> 00:24:27,032
The press release

474
00:24:27,098 --> 00:24:28,033
sounds fine.

475
00:24:28,900 --> 00:24:29,901
Let's give it a shot.

476
00:24:31,570 --> 00:24:32,737
Thank you.

477
00:24:38,243 --> 00:24:39,444
-Mr. Nomura.
-Huh?

478
00:24:39,511 --> 00:24:42,581
Did you send Ms. Miyamoto
your draft questions for the runners?

479
00:24:42,647 --> 00:24:44,316
Oh, right.

480
00:24:46,418 --> 00:24:48,153
What a pain…

481
00:24:50,021 --> 00:24:52,557
Sorry, Miyamoto.

482
00:24:52,624 --> 00:24:55,293
Can the questions for Yamanashi Gakuin

483
00:24:55,360 --> 00:24:56,394
wait a bit?

484
00:24:58,563 --> 00:25:01,099
No, you don't need to send them.

485
00:25:01,166 --> 00:25:02,000
What?

486
00:25:02,767 --> 00:25:04,603
Aren't you checking them?

487
00:25:05,570 --> 00:25:06,638
I'm not.

488
00:25:07,539 --> 00:25:08,874
You're not?

489
00:25:10,041 --> 00:25:12,010
I'll leave it to you, Mr. Nomura.

490
00:25:12,077 --> 00:25:13,578
Leave it to me?

491
00:25:14,145 --> 00:25:15,013
Yes.

492
00:25:19,084 --> 00:25:20,218
Okay then.

493
00:25:22,187 --> 00:25:23,021
See you.

494
00:25:23,088 --> 00:25:25,423
-Good work.
-Good work.

495
00:25:25,490 --> 00:25:27,259
-Good work.
-Oh.

496
00:25:27,759 --> 00:25:30,328
Mr. Tokushige, did you see this?

497
00:25:30,395 --> 00:25:31,363
What is it?

498
00:25:31,429 --> 00:25:33,265
A press release
from the Student United Team.

499
00:25:33,999 --> 00:25:35,066
Huh?

500
00:25:35,700 --> 00:25:36,835
It's rare

501
00:25:36,902 --> 00:25:39,304
for the Student United Team
to put out something like this.

502
00:25:39,371 --> 00:25:41,406
Their enthusiasm is great and all,

503
00:25:41,473 --> 00:25:43,675
but is this even news?

504
00:25:43,742 --> 00:25:46,811
The Student United Team is just
a thrown-together bunch of weeds.

505
00:25:46,878 --> 00:25:48,513
Weeds…

506
00:25:48,580 --> 00:25:50,315
Could you drop by their practice?

507
00:25:50,382 --> 00:25:52,517
What, go there?

508
00:25:52,584 --> 00:25:54,986
I'm swamped with other interviews.

509
00:25:55,053 --> 00:25:56,021
Can you go?

510
00:25:56,087 --> 00:25:58,256
-The one on the list--
-No.

511
00:25:58,323 --> 00:26:00,091
The Student United Team

512
00:26:00,158 --> 00:26:01,826
passes the sash too.

513
00:26:03,028 --> 00:26:04,296
Understood.

514
00:26:04,362 --> 00:26:05,697
Which director should go with him?

515
00:26:06,264 --> 00:26:07,599
Let's see…

516
00:26:08,500 --> 00:26:09,534
I'll go!

517
00:26:10,135 --> 00:26:11,536
Wait, will Toyama be okay?

518
00:26:13,271 --> 00:26:14,105
Please go.

519
00:26:15,073 --> 00:26:16,241
Okay!

520
00:26:20,612 --> 00:26:22,714
Run! Don't lose your rhythm!

521
00:26:22,781 --> 00:26:25,483
Next, how do you feel
heading into the main race?

522
00:26:26,117 --> 00:26:29,854
We have great talent
on this year's Student United Team.

523
00:26:29,921 --> 00:26:31,790
I think we'll get results
we can be happy with.

524
00:26:32,424 --> 00:26:33,458
By the way, Coach Kai,

525
00:26:34,392 --> 00:26:37,362
no one's ever started a coaching career

526
00:26:37,429 --> 00:26:39,364
with the Student United Team.

527
00:26:39,431 --> 00:26:40,365
That's right.

528
00:26:40,899 --> 00:26:44,369
It's your first time
leading a team as head coach.

529
00:26:45,003 --> 00:26:48,106
And you have to unite runners
from different schools.

530
00:26:48,807 --> 00:26:50,108
That must be really tough.

531
00:26:50,675 --> 00:26:52,344
It's a challenge worth taking.

532
00:26:53,244 --> 00:26:54,179
I'll make this team

533
00:26:55,380 --> 00:26:56,681
unlike any before.

534
00:26:57,515 --> 00:26:58,550
I see.

535
00:26:59,451 --> 00:27:01,252
Like never before.

536
00:27:01,853 --> 00:27:03,421
Well, I'm looking forward to it.

537
00:27:04,789 --> 00:27:07,258
-Stop the camera.
-Oh, right.

538
00:27:08,326 --> 00:27:11,329
Next, we'll interview the runners.

539
00:27:11,396 --> 00:27:12,530
-Thank you.
-Let's go.

540
00:27:12,597 --> 00:27:13,465
Okay.

541
00:27:16,701 --> 00:27:20,105
That guy's looking down on us.

542
00:27:20,672 --> 00:27:22,440
Let people who want to think that

543
00:27:22,507 --> 00:27:23,808
go on thinking it.

544
00:27:24,843 --> 00:27:27,445
It'll make things more fun.

545
00:27:28,847 --> 00:27:30,048
You'll see.

546
00:27:31,116 --> 00:27:32,150
You jerk!

547
00:27:34,853 --> 00:27:36,021
We're rolling!

548
00:27:36,688 --> 00:27:38,156
Um…

549
00:27:39,090 --> 00:27:40,725
from Toho University of Economics…

550
00:27:42,260 --> 00:27:43,461
Daichi Murai.

551
00:27:45,797 --> 00:27:48,833
You're from Akita, right, Mr. Sawada?

552
00:27:48,900 --> 00:27:49,901
Aomori.

553
00:27:50,669 --> 00:27:52,370
Sorry, Aomori.

554
00:27:52,437 --> 00:27:53,905
Well, it's all Tohoku.

555
00:27:53,972 --> 00:27:56,441
That took longer than I thought.

556
00:27:57,509 --> 00:27:59,411
That's enough on the Student United Team.

557
00:27:59,477 --> 00:28:00,745
We got the runners' comments.

558
00:28:00,812 --> 00:28:02,447
Sorry!

559
00:28:02,514 --> 00:28:05,116
I think I left the lens cap! I'll go back!

560
00:28:05,183 --> 00:28:07,719
Oh, yeah?
Then sorry, I'll head back first.

561
00:28:07,786 --> 00:28:08,620
What?

562
00:28:08,687 --> 00:28:09,554
Good work.

563
00:28:10,155 --> 00:28:10,989
To Dainichi TV.

564
00:28:14,526 --> 00:28:15,894
What?

565
00:28:19,831 --> 00:28:20,799
It's not here.

566
00:29:00,071 --> 00:29:01,606
I'm sorry! I filmed you without asking.

567
00:29:03,441 --> 00:29:05,744
Um, you're from Dainichi TV…

568
00:29:06,311 --> 00:29:07,378
I'm Toyama.

569
00:29:08,079 --> 00:29:10,081
I forgot something.

570
00:29:13,518 --> 00:29:14,886
Hold on a second.

571
00:29:18,289 --> 00:29:19,624
‎This one…

572
00:29:23,194 --> 00:29:25,230
-Is this it?
-That's it!

573
00:29:25,797 --> 00:29:27,899
-Sorry. Thank you.
-I'm glad.

574
00:29:28,399 --> 00:29:29,367
Well, see you.

575
00:29:32,570 --> 00:29:35,774
I was there at the qualifying race!

576
00:29:36,574 --> 00:29:38,510
Meisei came so close.

577
00:29:40,078 --> 00:29:42,747
It was just ten seconds, right?

578
00:29:51,956 --> 00:29:53,758
We couldn't close…

579
00:29:55,860 --> 00:29:57,328
that ten-second gap…

580
00:30:03,701 --> 00:30:04,602
Well then…

581
00:30:16,214 --> 00:30:17,949
TO RUN

582
00:30:27,692 --> 00:30:28,593
What?

583
00:30:29,761 --> 00:30:30,795
I…

584
00:30:32,597 --> 00:30:34,132
messed up.

585
00:30:34,699 --> 00:30:35,533
What did you do?

586
00:30:36,434 --> 00:30:39,771
I said something insensitive today

587
00:30:40,405 --> 00:30:42,106
to Aoba from Meisei Gakuin.

588
00:30:43,208 --> 00:30:44,309
What did you say?

589
00:30:45,944 --> 00:30:47,045
I said it was a shame

590
00:30:48,146 --> 00:30:51,583
they missed by just ten seconds.

591
00:30:53,651 --> 00:30:55,086
And he said,

592
00:30:56,054 --> 00:31:01,192
"We couldn't close that ten-second gap."

593
00:31:03,094 --> 00:31:06,631
For them, ten seconds is enormous.

594
00:31:07,498 --> 00:31:11,903
I can't forget the look on his face.

595
00:31:13,771 --> 00:31:16,341
It wasn't just frustration.

596
00:31:17,041 --> 00:31:20,712
He looked like he was really hurting.

597
00:31:24,883 --> 00:31:25,950
That's because

598
00:31:27,185 --> 00:31:28,953
he's not running alone, Toyama.

599
00:31:29,587 --> 00:31:30,555
What?

600
00:31:30,622 --> 00:31:32,957
A Hakone Ekiden team
has ten runners, right?

601
00:31:34,425 --> 00:31:37,295
What runners care about most

602
00:31:38,263 --> 00:31:41,833
is passing the sash to the next runner.

603
00:31:44,535 --> 00:31:47,639
His poor run at the qualifying race

604
00:31:48,406 --> 00:31:50,308
kept his team out of Hakone.

605
00:31:51,743 --> 00:31:54,646
In other words, it's his fault

606
00:31:54,712 --> 00:31:57,615
his teammates couldn't pass the sash.

607
00:31:57,682 --> 00:32:01,219
That's what he probably thinks.

608
00:32:04,722 --> 00:32:07,892
That makes what I said even worse.

609
00:32:10,028 --> 00:32:12,130
But isn't that okay?

610
00:32:14,666 --> 00:32:17,669
You noticed it and reflected on it.

611
00:32:19,003 --> 00:32:21,506
Now just use that next time.

612
00:32:24,309 --> 00:32:27,979
That's the first time
you've ever praised me.

613
00:32:28,046 --> 00:32:30,415
-I wasn't praising you.
-Thank you!

614
00:32:30,481 --> 00:32:32,817
I'm so motivated right now!

615
00:32:34,052 --> 00:32:35,186
I'll knock out

616
00:32:35,253 --> 00:32:37,355
your insanely long list of brutal fixes

617
00:32:37,422 --> 00:32:38,756
in no time!

618
00:32:38,823 --> 00:32:40,224
Oh, okay.

619
00:32:41,025 --> 00:32:43,962
-Okay, then. I'll leave the rest to you.
-Okay!

620
00:32:53,471 --> 00:32:54,672
Man,

621
00:32:54,739 --> 00:32:58,109
today's Dainichi TV interview
got me so pumped!

622
00:32:58,176 --> 00:33:01,746
I told them to film me from the right,

623
00:33:01,813 --> 00:33:04,882
but it was kind of more from the front.

624
00:33:04,949 --> 00:33:06,684
Don't worry. Nobody's going to watch it.

625
00:33:06,751 --> 00:33:07,852
Hey.

626
00:33:08,353 --> 00:33:10,254
Captain, what did they ask you?

627
00:33:11,889 --> 00:33:13,291
They asked about our team goal

628
00:33:13,958 --> 00:33:15,193
and…

629
00:33:15,793 --> 00:33:17,895
how I feel going into the main race.

630
00:33:17,962 --> 00:33:21,833
Whoa. You told them the team goal?

631
00:33:24,836 --> 00:33:26,337
-Was that bad?
-No.

632
00:33:27,605 --> 00:33:30,074
I'm just amazed you could say it.

633
00:33:31,442 --> 00:33:33,344
Top three and all.

634
00:33:34,812 --> 00:33:37,015
-What's that supposed to mean?
-No.

635
00:33:37,615 --> 00:33:38,616
Nothing.

636
00:33:42,954 --> 00:33:45,089
I've been thinking this since yesterday.

637
00:33:46,057 --> 00:33:49,093
Haven't you four been rude all along?

638
00:33:51,562 --> 00:33:52,730
I thought so too.

639
00:33:53,398 --> 00:33:55,133
You're ignoring Coach Kai's instructions.

640
00:33:55,199 --> 00:33:56,367
Of course we are.

641
00:33:56,434 --> 00:34:00,071
You'd have to be crazy
to take this training seriously!

642
00:34:00,138 --> 00:34:01,773
I agree.

643
00:34:01,839 --> 00:34:05,643
Nobody's heard of the Student United Team
having camp every week.

644
00:34:07,612 --> 00:34:08,946
He's never even been a head coach.

645
00:34:09,013 --> 00:34:11,849
I think he's just dragging us
into his crazy dream.

646
00:34:12,984 --> 00:34:13,851
First of all,

647
00:34:14,419 --> 00:34:16,487
what does that coach know about us?

648
00:34:21,225 --> 00:34:23,428
What exactly are you unhappy about?

649
00:34:25,096 --> 00:34:27,231
He hasn't even explained
his coaching philosophy.

650
00:34:28,166 --> 00:34:31,602
I want to spend a year aiming
for next year's Hakone.

651
00:34:33,337 --> 00:34:34,639
A top-three finish

652
00:34:35,406 --> 00:34:36,841
with this team is unrealistic.

653
00:34:37,542 --> 00:34:39,744
The team's times aren't official.

654
00:34:41,646 --> 00:34:42,947
No official standings either.

655
00:34:43,548 --> 00:34:44,382
I'm fine

656
00:34:44,882 --> 00:34:48,352
as long as
I run a race I'm satisfied with.

657
00:34:49,220 --> 00:34:51,756
If I get injured from overtraining,
what's the point?

658
00:34:51,823 --> 00:34:52,824
But…

659
00:34:53,791 --> 00:34:57,462
you're all here
because you want to run Hakone, right?

660
00:34:58,162 --> 00:35:01,199
Once you've decided that,
shouldn't you follow the coach?

661
00:35:01,265 --> 00:35:02,333
No.

662
00:35:04,836 --> 00:35:07,238
The whole point of the Student United Team

663
00:35:08,906 --> 00:35:12,443
is to let as many runners as possible

664
00:35:12,510 --> 00:35:14,812
experience Hakone, right?

665
00:35:16,380 --> 00:35:17,582
So isn't it enough

666
00:35:17,648 --> 00:35:21,886
if each of us
runs a race we're happy with?

667
00:35:21,953 --> 00:35:23,354
This team stuff

668
00:35:24,555 --> 00:35:27,091
is beside the point!

669
00:35:28,993 --> 00:35:32,830
If we're going to run,
shouldn't we take it seriously?

670
00:35:33,664 --> 00:35:34,765
Right.

671
00:35:34,832 --> 00:35:37,235
In the end, that's what's good for you.

672
00:35:37,902 --> 00:35:39,036
Spare me the pretty speeches.

673
00:35:45,076 --> 00:35:47,044
-Um…
-Yes?

674
00:35:48,946 --> 00:35:50,882
If you don't like it, why not just quit?

675
00:35:52,650 --> 00:35:55,019
You're dragging us down
when you just jog along.

676
00:35:57,788 --> 00:36:00,925
I don't want to hear that
from a guy with a 29-minute best.

677
00:36:03,394 --> 00:36:04,328
-Say that again.
-Jo!

678
00:36:04,395 --> 00:36:05,363
-Jo!
-I'll say it again.

679
00:36:05,429 --> 00:36:07,165
Don't wanna hear that
from a 29-minute guy.

680
00:36:07,231 --> 00:36:09,400
Think slacking off in practice is cool?

681
00:36:09,467 --> 00:36:11,002
Don't you have any pride as a runner?

682
00:36:11,068 --> 00:36:12,770
-Back at you.
-Huh?

683
00:36:12,837 --> 00:36:14,772
You're getting cocky
over a ridiculous goal.

684
00:36:15,740 --> 00:36:17,975
So who's the one with no pride?

685
00:36:18,042 --> 00:36:19,744
Don't mess with me!

686
00:36:19,810 --> 00:36:21,379
-I'm not!
-Knock it off, Jo!

687
00:36:21,445 --> 00:36:23,114
-Both of you…
-Stop it!

688
00:36:23,181 --> 00:36:25,016
-…calm down!
-Break it up!

689
00:36:30,655 --> 00:36:32,156
At the recent qualifying race…

690
00:36:36,427 --> 00:36:37,495
It was ten seconds.

691
00:36:41,432 --> 00:36:43,034
If we'd cut just ten seconds,

692
00:36:45,269 --> 00:36:46,971
our school would've made Hakone.

693
00:36:54,345 --> 00:36:56,147
I don't want it to end like this!

694
00:36:58,349 --> 00:36:59,283
I can't let it!

695
00:37:01,652 --> 00:37:03,187
Isn't it the same for all of you?

696
00:37:05,790 --> 00:37:07,825
You couldn't run Hakone
with your teammates.

697
00:37:08,960 --> 00:37:13,064
It hurt,
and that's why you came here, right?

698
00:37:15,633 --> 00:37:17,635
Sure, the Student United Team

699
00:37:18,202 --> 00:37:19,804
gets no record and no position.

700
00:37:20,605 --> 00:37:22,640
Maybe our sash doesn't carry much weight.

701
00:37:24,175 --> 00:37:25,009
But…

702
00:37:28,045 --> 00:37:29,046
even so,

703
00:37:30,581 --> 00:37:32,416
I want to run Hakone with this team!

704
00:37:36,554 --> 00:37:38,756
We've lost once already!

705
00:37:40,391 --> 00:37:42,193
That's exactly why
we can run like no one else!

706
00:37:44,295 --> 00:37:45,596
I think Coach Kai…

707
00:37:48,499 --> 00:37:51,068
truly believes in guys like us.

708
00:37:56,407 --> 00:37:57,942
I want to run Hakone!

709
00:38:00,911 --> 00:38:01,879
With this team

710
00:38:03,581 --> 00:38:04,949
and with Coach Kai.

711
00:38:08,853 --> 00:38:09,820
Captain.

712
00:38:11,689 --> 00:38:13,591
I get…

713
00:38:15,393 --> 00:38:16,427
how you feel.

714
00:38:19,797 --> 00:38:23,134
But we're going to do things

715
00:38:24,435 --> 00:38:25,603
our way.

716
00:39:13,417 --> 00:39:14,251
Haruka.

717
00:39:16,921 --> 00:39:19,890
I was looking for you.
There's something I want to show you.

718
00:39:23,327 --> 00:39:24,161
Here.

719
00:39:24,228 --> 00:39:25,563
ACHILLES TENDON STRETCHES

720
00:39:25,629 --> 00:39:26,664
Stretches?

721
00:39:27,264 --> 00:39:29,800
You hurt your left Achilles tendon
once as a second-year,

722
00:39:29,867 --> 00:39:33,137
and again before the qualifying race.

723
00:39:35,306 --> 00:39:39,443
Coach Kai said to get in good shape
for Hakone without overdoing it.

724
00:39:41,912 --> 00:39:44,548
I found some good videos.
I'll send them to you.

725
00:39:46,884 --> 00:39:48,853
Haruka! Stay! Don't speed up now!

726
00:39:49,520 --> 00:39:52,156
…35, 36, 37…

727
00:39:53,991 --> 00:39:54,859
How does

728
00:39:56,060 --> 00:39:57,294
Coach know

729
00:39:57,361 --> 00:39:58,796
about my old injuries?

730
00:40:00,498 --> 00:40:02,733
Before camp, Coach Kai apparently visited

731
00:40:02,800 --> 00:40:06,203
the head coach at every school.

732
00:40:07,004 --> 00:40:09,407
-Every school? All 15?
-Yes.

733
00:40:09,940 --> 00:40:11,942
Not just your running style

734
00:40:12,009 --> 00:40:16,113
and your health.
He looked into personal things too.

735
00:40:17,014 --> 00:40:19,917
Like your family background
and what foods you like.

736
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:24,255
He said he wanted to really get to know

737
00:40:24,755 --> 00:40:27,057
each and every runner.

738
00:40:43,974 --> 00:40:46,076
‎TOHO ECONOMICS

739
00:42:01,752 --> 00:42:02,620
Um…

740
00:42:08,192 --> 00:42:11,362
I don't want it to end like this.

741
00:42:16,734 --> 00:42:19,803
I want to run for real…

742
00:42:23,874 --> 00:42:25,309
with the Student United Team too.

743
00:42:35,953 --> 00:42:38,188
Captain, you're way too early!

744
00:42:40,858 --> 00:42:42,626
Huh? Haruka?

745
00:42:43,227 --> 00:42:45,162
-Morning.
-Good morning.

746
00:42:46,430 --> 00:42:47,731
Morning.

747
00:42:47,798 --> 00:42:48,899
What are you two

748
00:42:48,966 --> 00:42:50,467
doing out here so early?

749
00:42:51,402 --> 00:42:52,236
Well…

750
00:42:52,836 --> 00:42:55,673
We just kind of decided to run together.

751
00:42:56,640 --> 00:42:59,577
Just kind of?

752
00:42:59,643 --> 00:43:02,146
Well…

753
00:43:02,646 --> 00:43:05,249
All right! Everyone, let's warm up!

754
00:43:05,316 --> 00:43:06,283
Warm up!

755
00:43:06,850 --> 00:43:09,787
Why are you suddenly shouting? We know.

756
00:43:09,853 --> 00:43:11,589
Let's do it.

757
00:43:12,189 --> 00:43:13,557
It felt like everything

758
00:43:13,624 --> 00:43:15,659
was on my side today.

759
00:43:15,726 --> 00:43:17,695
My running was perfect too.

760
00:43:19,730 --> 00:43:22,399
50 DAYS UNTIL HAKONE EKIDEN

761
00:43:22,466 --> 00:43:24,234
I just watched it.

762
00:43:24,301 --> 00:43:26,503
Toyama's video is a lot better, right?

763
00:43:26,570 --> 00:43:27,938
I'd give it 70 points.

764
00:43:28,672 --> 00:43:30,874
I'll make revision notes for the other 30.

765
00:43:32,376 --> 00:43:33,577
‎REVISION NOTES

766
00:43:33,644 --> 00:43:34,878
More notes again?

767
00:43:34,945 --> 00:43:36,146
Revision notes?

768
00:43:36,213 --> 00:43:38,582
Whether it's a website video or broadcast,

769
00:43:38,649 --> 00:43:40,684
it should be done the same way.

770
00:43:40,751 --> 00:43:42,086
I just sent it to Toyama.

771
00:43:42,586 --> 00:43:44,788
Trusting people is different

772
00:43:44,855 --> 00:43:46,624
from giving up on them.

773
00:43:47,257 --> 00:43:49,994
But don't scare her too much, okay?

774
00:43:52,663 --> 00:43:53,664
But I think Toyama

775
00:43:54,465 --> 00:43:56,367
is cut out for Hakone.

776
00:43:57,501 --> 00:43:58,335
What's this?

777
00:43:59,436 --> 00:44:00,904
STICK WITH IT!

778
00:44:02,106 --> 00:44:03,607
A natto roll.

779
00:44:04,174 --> 00:44:06,677
-What does that mean?
-Nothing.

780
00:44:07,578 --> 00:44:09,346
What is that?

781
00:44:24,128 --> 00:44:25,729
Hello? What's up?

782
00:44:29,466 --> 00:44:30,334
What?

783
00:44:31,402 --> 00:44:32,636
Okay, got it.

784
00:44:33,370 --> 00:44:35,239
Call me as soon as you know the details.

785
00:44:37,174 --> 00:44:38,008
Yeah.

786
00:44:39,710 --> 00:44:40,778
Is something wrong?

787
00:44:43,113 --> 00:44:45,215
Maeda will probably be hospitalized.

788
00:44:45,282 --> 00:44:47,518
‎KANTO UNIVERSITY

789
00:44:51,855 --> 00:44:53,190
This is bad…

790
00:44:56,026 --> 00:44:57,027
50 DAYS TO HAKONE EKIDEN

791
00:44:57,094 --> 00:44:57,961
THIS DRAMA IS A WORK OF FICTION.

792
00:44:58,028 --> 00:44:59,530
Honestly,

793
00:44:59,596 --> 00:45:02,166
-this broadcast is in real trouble.
-I said this isn't a joke.

794
00:45:02,232 --> 00:45:03,967
Can I have a word?

795
00:45:04,034 --> 00:45:04,902
Subtitle translation by: Ken Nishizaki
